says we just heard families across the  country are facing new challenges  as this pandemic forces many students to  begin the school year remotely  hopefully they're not using deaths that  look like they were from the last  century  we met stay-at-home mom her name is  kieran walia and her two sons their name  ishan  and arman so they are starting high  school and the fifth grade from their  porter ranch  that's in california the family shared  their daily challenges and triumphs with  us  as they try to navigate this new normal  of remote learning  are you ready to wake up yeah no this is  what our typical morning looks like  these unprecedented times have created a  new  norm ishan is in the middle of his zoom  meeting  in preparation for his freshman year in  high school  i'm just nervous not about going into a  new school but  how the teachers adapted to online  learning  armand is working diligently on his  math homework what i like about doing  school from home  is that my teacher gives nice fabulous  comments about me  the worst part of online learning is  that i can't see my teacher  i can't play with my friends and i  don't have research or lunch how's your  lunch  [Music]  i can sense that the kids feel the  isolation  mom it's your turn i am definitely  concerned that the kids are not learning  enough  in virtual school i am scared  it's a very difficult time  we have to keep the kids positive we  have to keep them  motivated we have to teach them not to  be afraid  [Music]  and that's a that's a portrait of one  family's attempt to learn  remotely we're going to talk to sal khan  now i want to welcome him in he's the  founder and ceo of khan academy that's a  non-profit  educational organization that provides  free online lessons for children of all  ages and also resources for parents and  teachers i've used them they're  wonderful you should check it out  he joins us now saul good morning i want  to give  parents some concrete tips for kids of  of all ages but  let's begin by just setting expectations  we know that online learning is not as  good as in-person learning  but can we do it better than we've been  doing it  yeah i think and we have to do it better  because this this looks like it's going  to be protracted school closures  and so the key is is not to set  expectations too high because that'll  just set everyone up for a stress and  disappointment and this is a time where  we have enough things to stress about  especially for parents if parents are  stressed out  then kids are going to feel stressed out  and they're going to have a harder time  learning  so the key thing is focus on the basics  obviously everyone school is supporting  them  to different degrees but especially if  your kids can get their math  their reading the writing depending on  the age of the student it can be  anywhere from 20 minutes a day  up to maybe two sessions or three  sessions of 20 or 30 minutes a day  if you can get that under your belt and  then you get your legs under you then  you can think about layering on more  subjects  yeah okay so let's talk about those  students for the younger kids grade  ages three to seven what should the  parental strategy be  for making sure they don't fall behind  yeah and this you know a lot of times  when we talk about distant school we  imagine school is first and foremost  about learning the academic standards  and it is that is what we associate  school for  but as you just saw in that last segment  school is also about the connections the  social emotional learning the connection  with the teacher  and so everything you do for your child  or if you're a teacher for the student  is try to optimize those connections so  i would say for a very young student we  have early learning app khan academy  kids it's free it's non-commercial all  funded by philanthropy  kids can work on that for 20 minutes a  day ideally they're doing that next to a  parent they're able to  still stay connected while they're doing  distance learning hopefully their  teachers are able to do a couple of  20-minute zoom sessions or video  conference sessions  every day and then those should be  pulling the students out of the screen  as much as possible getting them to do  interactive things especially for that  younger age group getting them to move  around even  yeah what about kids there in the middle  maybe ages 8 to 13. you know the  challenge there isn't getting them  online it's getting them off of the  video games and talking to their friends  and focusing on school what's the  strategy for those guys  yeah you know and i i have three kids  eleven nine and five so i'm kind of  you know straddling these age brackets  and you're absolutely right i think the  key there is  try to set goals with them try to  understand what's achievable in the day  a lot of those teachers who are doing  this well are doing exactly that  they're setting goals for the day  they're setting goals for the week  they're doing  regular check-ins and when they get in  on google hangout or google meet or they  get on zoom or whatever video conference  they're using  instead of it just being about a one-way  lecture-based interaction  they've got to really pull them out ask  them questions ask them to work on video  conference breakouts  do some goal setting with them do some  small group sessions and not just  between the student and the teacher as  you heard from the last segment it's  super important that students are able  to interact with each other as well  and if you're not seeing that from your  child's school try to get some of that  outside of whatever is being provided  you could do socially distance play  dates in the park whatever especially  i would say all ages that social  interaction even for us adults  is super important yeah  all right so thank you very much and i  want to say that it's great to hear your  voice live i heard it uh remotely uh on  one of your lessons just last week  you were giving me a tutorial to help my  fifth grader on order of operations  always tricky for a rising sixth grader  if you didn't pay attention in class  anyway thank you very much anthony  tossing it back to you  all right thank you both
 

